ust go  huh  okay let's go it again and she never  lost her  her joy  of acting she never came down on herself  she never she never uh  many actors after that something  happened so go oh  and she never did that and he was  shocked he'd never seen it  so i would say  you know 90 percent or 99  maybe of actors are very hard on  themselves and then those sparkling  one percent or 10 percent are are  unusual  but of course they have so much freedom  and of course you want  actors to have that kind of freedom and  i've taught actors for a long time to  acting classes and i always wanted them  to have that freedom to forgive  themselves to  to uh and that that's why i say to  directors  you know permission to fail is the most  valuable thing that you can give an  actor is you you can tell them  uh  don't worry we'll do it again if if i  won't use anything that doesn't work  so um  we have all the time in the world and  even telling an actor we have all the  time in the world even if you don't  you'll actually get the shot faster than  if you uh say to an actor okay we've  only got one more we only got time for  one more shot so we've got to get it on  this shot  it it's actually less likely to go well  than if you say you know we've got all  the time in the world let's just do one  more  and uh you're more likely to get  something good out of out of that  permission to fail that it's okay if you  fail  would you see empathy  um the degrees of empathy  maybe blossom more let's say there's a  scale and when a student would come to  you in the past uh for the the classes  that in you know the workshops uh that  their empathy they were closed off and  you could see it in them whether it was  family conditioning and then over time  the scale that they came more to this  side where  they just were open open to themselves  open to other people well that was  always my hope of course  it's always my hope  i i try not to make myself responsible  for  whether that happens or not b